WEBVTT

00:00:01.390 --> 00:00:05.979
Twenty to ten at night. Someone is on your team's website, and they have questions.

00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:18.572
They open Ask Pulse. On the right, everything ONE MOVE will receive, filling in as they talk.

00:00:26.290 --> 00:00:28.921
First, how long is the drive from their street to downtown?

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:39.996
Pulse looks up the address and answers: about nineteen miles, twenty-six minutes, longer at rush hour, and the train as well. A real answer, not ask an agent.

00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:53.159
Then the going price of a home in Draper. Area figures are public, so Pulse gives them, with the source and the year.

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:04.329
Crime questions get the published figures and the year, never a judgement about the area.

00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:18.180
Then the big one: what is my home worth? Pulse never guesses a number. It starts gathering what an agent needs for a CMA.

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:26.572
Bedrooms, bathrooms and square feet.

00:01:33.550 --> 00:01:36.018
The lot, the year it was built, and what they have updated.

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:49.852
When they want to move, and what comes next. Then Pulse makes the promise: one of our top agents will prepare a detailed CMA.

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:02.287
Their name, mobile and email, and a monthly report on their street if they want one.

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:14.606
One tap, and it is sent. The lead, every answer and every detail, in ONE MOVE in under a minute.

00:02:20.110 --> 00:02:24.750
By seven the next morning, it is waiting for you. You check it, set the price, and send the CMA.

00:02:30.330 --> 00:02:32.681
Chapter one. Your site, your way.

00:02:38.010 --> 00:02:43.746
Start from one of forty templates, made for an agent, a team or an office, each shown with your own name and photo.

00:02:53.250 --> 00:02:55.494
Filter by who it is for, or by the look you like.

00:03:03.550 --> 00:03:06.111
Pick one, and it opens in the builder, ready to change.

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:18.129
Change anything you see. Click the heading, and type your own words.

00:03:26.010 --> 00:03:29.526
Make it bigger, or bolder. Every word, every size, every space.

00:03:35.690 --> 00:03:38.233
Swap the fonts and the colours for the whole site in one place.

00:03:45.910 --> 00:03:50.138
Add a new section wherever you want it: listings, reviews, your areas, a contact form.

00:03:56.610 --> 00:03:58.694
See it as a phone shows it, before anyone else does.

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:14.859
Some things never change: your brokerage, your licence, the Equal Housing line, the MLS credits and the consent wording. They stay put on every page.

00:04:21.820 --> 00:04:27.341
Press Publish. ONE checks every page for anything hard to read, lists it for you, and then it is live.

00:04:32.490 --> 00:04:34.395
Chapter two. Build with Pulse.

00:04:42.340 --> 00:04:44.588
Or just tell Pulse what you want, in plain words.

00:04:51.860 --> 00:04:54.795
Ask for three versions, and Pulse gives you three different ways to do it.

00:05:01.090 --> 00:05:05.165
Pick one. Pulse changes only your draft, and one Undo takes it all back.

00:05:09.240 --> 00:05:14.324
Fifty asks a month. Changing anything by hand is always free, and nothing goes live until you press Publish.

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:22.924
Chapter three. Finding a home.

00:05:27.040 --> 00:05:29.544
Your team's listings, every agent's, in one place.

00:05:36.370 --> 00:05:38.782
Visitors can describe the home they want in their own words.

00:05:45.480 --> 00:05:49.865
Pulse shows what it understood, and the homes that fit, each with what matched and the drive.

00:06:00.120 --> 00:06:03.989
Every home gets its own page: the photos, the story and the facts.

00:06:13.340 --> 00:06:17.700
Where the listing has a 3D tour, it plays right on the page. Drag to look around.

00:06:25.210 --> 00:06:29.520
The photos, the story, and a monthly payment estimate that updates as they change the numbers.

00:06:36.670 --> 00:06:39.005
And the drive from this front door to their own work.

00:06:46.890 --> 00:06:51.099
Buyers can see what it would take to buy it: the cash to close and the monthly payment.

00:06:59.980 --> 00:07:08.301
They type their own numbers, and every line updates as they go: lender fees, title, taxes, insurance, the deposit and any seller credit.

00:07:15.780 --> 00:07:22.205
Or turn it round: what can I afford? Their income, their debts and their savings give a comfortable price and a stretch.

00:07:29.280 --> 00:07:36.834
When the MLS feed is switched on, the same sites search every home for sale. This is Morgan's example site, with example homes.

00:07:42.620 --> 00:07:46.848
Search by the drive. Within twenty minutes of their work, typed in their own words.

00:07:55.520 --> 00:07:59.933
Only the homes inside a twenty-minute drive come back, on the list and on the map.

00:08:09.980 --> 00:08:14.500
Or draw the area on the map. Click a corner, then the next, all the way round.

00:08:24.280 --> 00:08:29.305
Search this area, and only the homes inside the lines are shown. One click clears it.

00:08:34.060 --> 00:08:36.857
Chapter four. Every area you serve.

00:08:39.860 --> 00:08:44.820
Every area you serve gets its own guide, written from public facts and kept up to date.

00:08:49.790 --> 00:08:53.592
The public schools near its centre, from the official lists, with no ratings.

00:08:59.520 --> 00:09:05.073
Who to call when you move in: the power, the water, the gas, the rubbish and the school district.

00:09:12.440 --> 00:09:15.992
How far to the ski resorts, and the team's own homes and agents in that area.

00:09:23.290 --> 00:09:32.020
And two ways to keep in touch: new listings by email, and a monthly report on their own street. Live market numbers join this page with the MLS feed.

00:09:39.850 --> 00:09:41.508
Chapter five. Selling a home.

00:09:45.650 --> 00:09:48.327
Your Sell page shows sellers everything you do, step by step.

00:09:53.700 --> 00:09:56.874
Every service has a See an example link, and the example is the real thing.

00:10:04.980 --> 00:10:06.601
The staging plan, room by room.

00:10:13.700 --> 00:10:16.611
Every offer laid side by side, with your read on each one.

00:10:26.220 --> 00:10:29.676
Every deadline from contract to close, and the closing day checklist.

00:10:36.610 --> 00:10:43.082
On the live site, Marquee's own example pages, ONE Open's room page and Showly's showing feedback open inside these examples too.

00:10:50.470 --> 00:10:53.984
And this is the CMA your seller receives, in your team's own look.

00:10:58.470 --> 00:11:05.667
It opens with your own two-minute welcome video. You record it once, with a script, and it plays on every CMA you send.

00:11:11.250 --> 00:11:13.721
Their own home's facts, checked against the county record.

00:11:19.160 --> 00:11:23.114
What sold near them, each sale adjusted to their home, against the range you set.

00:11:30.890 --> 00:11:33.040
Their competition, who is looking, and the price.

00:11:40.190 --> 00:11:49.574
They try a price. The slider shows what they would walk away with, and the homes in the report nearest that price, with each one's own days on the market. Never a forecast.

00:11:57.160 --> 00:12:00.308
Their net sheet is right in the report, and they can change any line.

00:12:08.890 --> 00:12:12.839
What to fix and what to skip, when to list, how you would sell it, and their next home.

00:12:19.790 --> 00:12:22.185
And one clear next step: book the walk-through.

00:12:38.000 --> 00:12:42.986
The net sheet also has its own page. Sellers type every line, and see the bottom line as they go.

00:12:52.460 --> 00:12:57.384
Behind it all, the agent picks the CMA's look from eight, with their own colours, fonts and photos.

00:13:04.810 --> 00:13:09.527
And records the welcome video once, with the script beside them, or uploads one they already have.

00:13:16.840 --> 00:13:18.994
Chapter six. Visitors who come back.

00:13:22.590 --> 00:13:26.879
Every agent on the team has a site of their own. On a listing there, a visitor can ask to know first.

00:13:35.800 --> 00:13:38.634
Their name and email, one tap, and they are watching the home.

00:13:46.380 --> 00:13:48.442
The moment the price drops, the email lands.

00:13:56.280 --> 00:13:58.378
One tap, and they are back on the home's page.

00:14:05.210 --> 00:14:11.384
Avery usually replies within eighteen minutes, and the page says so. Visitors can book a tour themselves from Avery's open times.

00:14:22.980 --> 00:14:30.623
They pick a day and a time, add their name, email and mobile, and send it. The time is held on Avery's own calendar until Avery confirms it.

00:14:38.280 --> 00:14:44.669
Get pre-approved, with Avery's own loan officer, named on the button. Name, email and mobile, never income or credit.

00:14:51.260 --> 00:14:56.419
The consent names the loan officer and is never ticked for them. Tick it, send it, and Riley reaches out.

00:15:02.570 --> 00:15:04.823
Pulse greets them with what fits the page they are on.

00:15:10.280 --> 00:15:14.048
And Pulse speaks Spanish. One tap on Español, and everything switches.

00:15:20.990 --> 00:15:26.110
Ask in Spanish, and the answer comes back in Spanish, with the same rules and the same care.

00:15:36.390 --> 00:15:40.071
Or read the whole page in Spanish. One tap on Español at the top.

00:15:46.310 --> 00:15:51.493
The legal lines stay exactly as they are, and the Equal Housing line is shown in Spanish beside the English.

00:15:57.310 --> 00:16:01.892
Chapter seven. For life: buyer, then owner, then the friend they send you.

00:16:09.500 --> 00:16:15.186
Once they are under contract, a buyer signs in to Your deal with the email you have for them. No password.

00:16:25.400 --> 00:16:28.855
A six-digit code arrives by email. They type it, and they are in.

00:16:34.840 --> 00:16:40.625
Every date in their contract, what comes next and how many days are left, with their agent one tap away.

00:16:47.980 --> 00:16:54.028
Years later, they own the home. My Home greets them on the anniversary and keeps everything about it in one place.

00:17:04.640 --> 00:17:10.960
Their own improvements log. They add what they did, when and what it cost, and choose whether their agent sees it.

00:17:19.980 --> 00:17:22.637
And a photo for each one, kept private to them.

00:17:30.940 --> 00:17:34.153
When you send them an updated market analysis, it is right here.

00:17:40.290 --> 00:17:47.394
And the report shows what changed since you sent it: a price change, a home under contract, one back on the market. Never a guess.

00:17:53.990 --> 00:18:01.076
One unticked box: tell me when my competition changes. They tick it, add their email, and they hear the day anything moves.

00:18:08.180 --> 00:18:10.724
Past clients get a page of their own: know someone moving?

00:18:22.700 --> 00:18:26.396
They tell you who, and their friend is contacted once, personally, by the agent.

00:18:33.890 --> 00:18:36.115
Chapter eight. Your blog writes itself.

00:18:39.740 --> 00:18:43.967
Switch on Autopilot, and Pulse writes a post about twice a week, from what people are reading right now.

00:18:51.820 --> 00:18:57.560
Each post is checked for its facts and its fair housing words before it goes up, and linked to the areas it names.

00:19:05.220 --> 00:19:09.719
A post that fails a check waits for you as a draft, and you can switch Pulse off at any time.

00:19:18.610 --> 00:19:24.207
Web Studio shows everything in one place: what needs you, this week's numbers, and everything Pulse did.

00:19:29.560 --> 00:19:34.063
Pulse wrote each page's Google title and description from the page's own words, with an Undo on every one.

00:19:39.970 --> 00:19:41.451
Chapter nine. Teams.

00:19:45.630 --> 00:19:47.575
A team gets one site with every agent on it.

00:19:55.460 --> 00:19:58.156
Visitors filter by area, and see the agents who work there.

00:20:05.890 --> 00:20:09.732
Each agent gets a page on the team site, with their listings and their reviews.

00:20:19.960 --> 00:20:25.202
And a site of their own. Add someone to the team in ONE, and both appear, with nothing to set up.

00:20:32.220 --> 00:20:35.993
Agents ask the team lead for an ad. The lead sees each request and answers it.

00:20:41.460 --> 00:20:42.828
A word back, and Make the ad.

00:20:47.100 --> 00:20:48.450
Chapter ten. ONE Growth.

00:20:52.710 --> 00:20:58.423
Pulse Ads, in four steps. Step one: what is the ad for? A new listing, an open house, a new price, and eighteen more.

00:21:06.220 --> 00:21:09.531
Pick the listing, and the ad gets its photo, its page and its street.

00:21:17.550 --> 00:21:22.521
Every word is checked for fair housing. A phrase like this one turns the check red before anyone sees it.

00:21:30.120 --> 00:21:34.411
Ask Pulse, and it writes the ad three ways, each one checked, with your brokerage named.

00:21:41.190 --> 00:21:42.603
Pick one, and the check turns green.

00:21:50.660 --> 00:21:56.835
Step three: where and how much. At least fifteen miles round the home, never by age, gender or ZIP code, and your own budget.

00:22:03.040 --> 00:22:05.055
Step four: you approve every word.

00:22:10.680 --> 00:22:15.215
It goes to your own Facebook ad account, paused, and you pay Facebook directly, at cost.

00:22:22.770 --> 00:22:28.873
The results screen shows what each source brought in: the leads, the appointments and the closings, for the team and for each agent.

00:22:37.850 --> 00:22:44.674
And Pulse results: which of Pulse's offers visitors say yes to, and which ones turn into leads. Keep what works.

00:22:49.060 --> 00:22:56.965
The site, the blog, the ads and the leads: one system. Your website, built on ONE. VIP fifty ONE dot com.
